# Provider Docker Rules

## Provider boundary

- Keep Docker concerns behind provider interfaces and adapter types.
- Map Docker/container-runtime errors into normalized control-plane errors.
- Do not expose Docker IDs, mount paths, or network names through public API models.

## Sandbox hardening requirements

- Run agent workloads as non-root with minimal capabilities.
- Enforce network isolation for agent containers (no inbound/outbound access).
- Restrict writable filesystem scope to workspace and bounded temp paths.

## Deployment and Portainer constraints

- Use internal network-only service communication by default.
- Route external access through Caddy TLS edge policy, not direct container port exposure.
- Treat Docker daemon access as privileged; use least-privilege endpoint approach where possible.

## Compose/deployment hygiene

- Keep environment variables explicit and minimal.
- Use immutable image tags for controlled promotion when possible.
- Include health/metrics exposure patterns consistent with lab monitoring stack.

## Change checklist

- If provider behavior changes, verify API contract remains provider-agnostic.
- If Docker access model changes, update ADRs and deployment docs in the same change.
